WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.429 --> 00:00:05.450
Sony Pictures Animation's Spider-Man: Into
the Spider-Verse is finally here, and it is,

00:00:05.450 --> 00:00:06.820
in a word, amazing.

00:00:07.160 --> 00:00:11.040
With a few tweaks, the next entry in the Spider-Verse
saga could be even better.

00:00:11.300 --> 00:00:15.780
Here are some aspects which the filmmakers
totally nailed, and some which left a little,

00:00:15.780 --> 00:00:17.840
very, very little, room for improvement.

00:00:17.960 --> 00:00:21.740
But be warned: some spectacularly minor spoilers
abound.

00:00:22.360 --> 00:00:24.900
The film's animation is pretty much incredible.

00:00:25.220 --> 00:00:29.340
It looks as if it were ripped from the pages
of a comic book, in more ways than one.

00:00:29.340 --> 00:00:33.640
Not only is the character design pretty much
perfect, but the film makes frequent, clever

00:00:33.640 --> 00:00:38.060
use of comic book tropes like dialogue boxes
and sound effects which are spelled out onscreen.

00:00:38.240 --> 00:00:42.900
At times, the animation straddles a fine line
between the styles of comic books and cartoons,

00:00:42.910 --> 00:00:48.090
which is altogether appropriate for a PG-rated
film featuring the first onscreen appearance

00:00:48.090 --> 00:00:53.450
of the Spectacular Spider-Ham, an alternate
version of Spidey who is a talking, anthropomorphic

00:00:53.450 --> 00:00:53.940
pig.

00:00:55.020 --> 00:00:57.260
"I just washed my hands, that's why they're
wet.

00:00:58.720 --> 00:00:59.480
No other reason."

00:00:59.700 --> 00:01:04.320
Into the Spider-Verse takes full advantage
of its medium, with knockout action sequences

00:01:04.320 --> 00:01:08.630
that would have been difficult, if not impossible,
to create in live action, and characters that

00:01:08.630 --> 00:01:10.320
simply wouldn't have worked otherwise.

00:01:10.840 --> 00:01:15.150
But for all that Into the Spider-Verse gets
right in this respect, there's one aesthetic

00:01:15.150 --> 00:01:18.460
choice that could have perhaps been reined
in a little bit.

00:01:18.920 --> 00:01:23.420
The filmmakers took the comics-inspired look
of the film to an extreme never before seen

00:01:23.420 --> 00:01:26.220
on film, and when it works, it really works.

00:01:26.600 --> 00:01:28.680
But when it doesn't, it becomes distracting.

00:01:29.220 --> 00:01:34.259
The film's backgrounds sometimes become intentionally
blurry, seemingly in order to help keep the

00:01:34.260 --> 00:01:36.210
viewer's focus in the middle of the screen.

00:01:36.480 --> 00:01:40.460
It's a strange contrast with the clean, sharp
lines of the characters and foregrounds, but,

00:01:40.470 --> 00:01:44.700
at times, it doesn't so much invoke comic
book art as it does threaten to give the viewer

00:01:44.700 --> 00:01:45.200
a headache.

00:01:45.900 --> 00:01:50.480
Into the Spider-Verse finally gives Spidey
fans the first big-screen appearance of Miles

00:01:50.490 --> 00:01:55.030
Morales, the Afro-Latino teen who took over
as Spider-Man in the now-defunct Ultimate

00:01:55.030 --> 00:01:59.640
universe before plot machinations transplanted
him into the mainstream Marvel continuity.

00:02:00.020 --> 00:02:05.040
The voice performance by Shameik Moore is
spot-on, expertly capturing Miles' awkwardness,

00:02:05.040 --> 00:02:07.080
humor, and determination.

00:02:07.200 --> 00:02:08.840
"Can Spider-Man turn invisible?"

00:02:08.960 --> 00:02:10.240
Not in my universe.

00:02:10.480 --> 00:02:11.720
You just poked me in my eye!

00:02:11.720 --> 00:02:14.140
This is incredible some kind of fright of flight thing?

00:02:14.140 --> 00:02:14.860
what's that?

00:02:16.900 --> 00:02:21.960
He makes every step of the teen's arc from
vulnerable kid to badass hero ring true, and

00:02:21.969 --> 00:02:26.919
he has an excellent chemistry with a pudgy,
schlubby alternate-universe Peter Parker,

00:02:26.920 --> 00:02:27.860
voiced by Jake Johnson.

00:02:27.980 --> 00:02:29.200
"Alright, time to swing,"

00:02:29.200 --> 00:02:29.980
Just like I taught you.

00:02:29.980 --> 00:02:31.100
When did you teach me that?

00:02:31.100 --> 00:02:32.980
I didn't.  it's a little joke.  Team building.

00:02:33.440 --> 00:02:37.700
But unfortunately, that chemistry also serves
to highlight one of the flick's other shortcomings.

00:02:38.320 --> 00:02:42.879
Into the Spider-Verse employs a hilarious
device to introduce all of its alternate Spideys.

00:02:43.240 --> 00:02:47.860
At the film's beginning, Peter Parker briefly
explains his origin and career as Spider-Man

00:02:47.860 --> 00:02:48.480
in a voiceover.

00:02:48.860 --> 00:02:50.280
"My name is Peter parker"

00:02:50.280 --> 00:02:52.280
I'm pretty sure you know the rest.

00:02:52.280 --> 00:02:55.220
I saved the city, fell in love, and then saved the city

00:02:55.220 --> 00:02:57.580
"again, and again, and again"

00:02:57.800 --> 00:03:02.120
It's simple, it's concise, and it's repeated
by each of the new Spideys as they're introduced

00:03:02.120 --> 00:03:02.620
to the film.

00:03:02.840 --> 00:03:07.060
This device is so light-hearted and funny
that it's easy to ignore how it effectively

00:03:07.060 --> 00:03:12.320
glosses over the origins and careers of these
other characters, but it does, and this kinda

00:03:12.320 --> 00:03:12.820
stinks.

00:03:12.980 --> 00:03:17.860
We miss out on learning about the universes
of Spider-Man Noir Peni Parker, and Spider-Ham.

00:03:18.340 --> 00:03:22.779
While the omissions make sense from a storytelling
standpoint, the movie is so fun that we can't

00:03:22.780 --> 00:03:23.780
help but want more.

00:03:24.109 --> 00:03:27.779
The characters shine during their brief time
on screen, but the truncated introductions

00:03:27.780 --> 00:03:31.260
end up leaving the alternate Spideys feeling
just a bit underdeveloped.

00:03:31.540 --> 00:03:33.940
Well, all of them, that is, except one.

00:03:35.160 --> 00:03:40.499
Voiced by Hailee Steinfeld, Into the Spider-Verse's
Gwen is everything fans could have hoped for,

00:03:40.500 --> 00:03:45.460
highly capable, and with the mix of compassionate
sarcasm that's a hallmark of any Spidey.

00:03:47.180 --> 00:03:47.700
"hey guys"

00:03:49.480 --> 00:03:49.980
Wanda?

00:03:50.160 --> 00:03:50.800
It's Gwen

00:03:51.300 --> 00:03:52.440
You know her?

00:03:52.820 --> 00:03:53.940
"very cool."

00:03:54.460 --> 00:03:58.160
Both the character and Steinfeld's performance
are appealing enough that it's no wonder Sony

00:03:58.170 --> 00:04:02.650
has greenlit an all-female Into the Spider-Verse
spin-off, in which Gwen will almost certainly

00:04:02.650 --> 00:04:03.140
take the lead.

00:04:03.709 --> 00:04:07.879
Sure, Into the Spider-Verse is now only just
hitting theaters, but Spider-Gwen's return

00:04:07.880 --> 00:04:10.240
to the big screen can't come soon enough.

00:04:11.060 --> 00:04:15.040
Into the Spider-Verse features more than one
villain, but discussing them all would venture

00:04:15.040 --> 00:04:17.440
a bit too far into spoiler territory.

00:04:17.440 --> 00:04:23.000
The film's Biggest Bad is Wilson Fisk, a.k.a.
the Kingpin, and while it's a perfectly acceptable

00:04:23.000 --> 00:04:27.730
choice, considering the character's long history
with Spidey, some fans might find that this

00:04:27.730 --> 00:04:30.240
version of Fisk leaves a bit to be desired.

00:04:30.580 --> 00:04:34.699
For starters, the character's rendering is
pretty distracting, as he is roughly the size

00:04:34.699 --> 00:04:39.400
and shape of a minivan, and literally shaped
like one, as well, easily four or five times

00:04:39.400 --> 00:04:41.980
larger than any other human character in the
film.

00:04:42.140 --> 00:04:46.680
Considering how ridiculous he looks in a movie
with an actual talking cartoon pig, it's actually

00:04:46.680 --> 00:04:47.660
kind of impressive.

00:04:48.320 --> 00:04:53.540
All that aside, Into the Spider-Verse remains
one of the very best superhero movies released

00:04:53.540 --> 00:04:54.540
in 2018.

00:04:54.560 --> 00:04:59.240
And with competition from the likes of Black
Panther, Deadpool 2, and Infinity War, that's

00:04:59.240 --> 00:05:00.380
really saying something.

00:05:00.680 --> 00:05:01.880
"Act super normal"

00:05:01.880 --> 00:05:03.120
selecting a bagel.

00:05:03.360 --> 00:05:03.860
Spider-Man?

00:05:04.020 --> 00:05:05.860
That's funny, I get that a lot.

00:05:08.520 --> 00:05:09.480
"he's got the bagel!"

